\begin{abstract}
Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong capabilities in generating source code across multiple programming languages. However, their outputs often exhibit distinctive stylistic and structural traits that differ from human-written code and vary across models. These differences carry concrete practical implications: in practice, security auditing needs motivate code provenance and stylistic analysis for attribution, reusability, and model selection guidance. Existing text-based detection methods face significant performance drops when directly applied to code attribution, due to fundamental differences between natural language and code semantics. To address the inadequacy of these methods, we propose the Code Generation Source Detection (CGSD) framework, which learns discriminative, style-sensitive embeddings via supervised contrastive learning with subsequent classification fine-tuning; similarity analysis and CodeStyleAnalyzer then quantify and interpret stylistic patterns. We conduct experiments on a self-constructed multi-class dataset comprising code generated by multiple mainstream LLMs. Results show that CGSD achieves 93.25\% attribution accuracy, significantly outperforming existing baseline methods, effectively capturing model-specific patterns and uncovering systematic stylistic divergences between models.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Large Language Models, Code Generation, Supervised Contrastive Learning, Stylistic Analysis
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

With the rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) in code generation, output quality now approaches expert-level performance~\citep{Coignion_2024,rasheed2024codepori}, improving productivity across research and industry. Automated coding helps teams focus on innovation and problem modeling rather than repetitive implementation.  

Due to budget, compute, and expertise constraints, teams often adopt cheaper or mixed-model pipelines, which in turn increases exposure to security risks. For instance, about 26\% of 452 GitHub Copilot snippets contained security vulnerabilities~\citep{FuCopilot2023}. This underscores the need for code provenance detection to enable rigorous security auditing and accountability, and it also motivates understanding stylistic differences in LLM-generated code—especially in mixed-model codebases where inconsistent styles hinder integration.

In these mixed-model settings, we observe consistent divergences in naming, comments, control flow, and exception handling. For example, Gemini tends to use \texttt{number}, whereas Claude consistently employs \texttt{num}. Such inconsistencies disrupt conventions, hinder reuse and reproducibility, and complicate integration. Systematic characterization enables source attribution and guides model selection.

Existing AIGC detection methods focus on general text~\citep{yangZeroShotDetectionMachineGenerated2023,baoFastDetectGPTEfficientZeroShot2023,mitchellDetectGPTZeroShotMachineGenerated2023}, lacking fine-grained code attribution. Code watermarking and authorship detection show promise~\citep{leeWhoWroteThis2024,sunIdiosyncrasiesLargeLanguage2025}. However, text-based classifiers underperform on code. Code has structural patterns such as syntax and control flow. It also contains domain-specific semantics, including idioms and conventions. In addition, stylistic signatures span naming, comments, and layout. Models trained on general text capture these aspects poorly. These limitations motivate domain-specific adaptations for effective LLM code source attribution.

