To address the failure of text-only models to capture code-specific patterns and stylistic signatures, we propose a \textbf{Code Generation Source Detection (CGSD)} framework that innovatively combines supervised contrastive learning with subsequent classification fine-tuning to achieve effective multi-model code attribution. The framework consists of three integrated components that collectively enable accurate source detection and stylistic characterization. As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:placeholder}, our approach integrates both training and analysis components:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Embedding-based Classification:} Learn a discriminative projection via supervised contrastive learning using a projection head to obtain a separable embedding space, then fine-tune a classification head for multi-class attribution.
    \item \textbf{Similarity Analysis:} Character- and word-level TF-IDF $n$-grams with cosine similarity, plus variable-naming Top-$k$ statistics and pairwise heatmaps to quantify inter-model stylistic relationships.
    \item \textbf{CodeStyleAnalyzer:} Proposed in this paper, it covers six dimensions—naming, comments, syntax, consistency, complexity: cyclomatic complexity, nesting depth, function length; and patterns: library usage, exception handling, return styles.
\end{enumerate}

This integrated framework not only enables accurate source attribution but also supports comprehensive characterization of stylistic traits across different LLM-generated codebases. We evaluate CGSD on a self-constructed, multi-model dataset sampled from CodeAlpaca prompts \citep{wangCodeAlpaca2023}, validating its effectiveness in both code provenance detection and model-specific pattern analysis.

Experimental results show distinct stylistic patterns across different LLMs. Our approach achieves 93.25\% attribution accuracy, outperforming LLM2Vec (60.20\%) and BERT (58.00\%) by nearly 30 percentage points.

\textbf{Our main contributions are:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose CGSD, a unified framework for LLM code source detection with embedding-based classification employing supervised contrastive learning and classification fine-tuning, integrating multi-class classification, similarity analysis, and stylistic quantification via CodeStyleAnalyzer.
    \item CGSD achieves 93.25\% attribution accuracy and reveals model-specific stylistic and structural patterns, improving interpretability and supporting code provenance applications. Performance far exceeds strong baselines, including a +33.05\% absolute improvement over LLM2Vec.
    \item We construct a high-quality, curated multi-model dataset for code provenance, spanning multiple mainstream LLMs and programming languages, paired on shared prompts with preprocessing. Upon publication, we will open-source the dataset and tooling, establishing a strong benchmark for future research.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
To systematically understand the characteristics of code generated by LLMs as well as related security and traceability issues, this section first reviews research methods in text generation detection that are relevant to model distinguishability. It then analyzes typical defect patterns in LLM-generated code, followed by an overview of existing code provenance and auditing techniques. Finally, it discusses the stylistic and distributional differences among various models in code generation.

\textbf{Distinguishability of LLM Outputs.} 
Previous studies on machine-generated text detection mainly follow two directions. 
The first approach is classifier-based training. Previous studies typically train binary classifiers to determine the source of generated text. 
Representative methods include graph-based or structured approaches~\cite{liu2022coco} and BERT fine-tuning~\cite{uchendu2021turingbenchbenchmarkenvironmentturing, ippolito2020automaticdetectiongeneratedtext}. 
The second direction leverages intrinsic properties in a zero-shot setting: by perturbing the text and analyzing log-likelihood curvature or sampling characteristics, the source model can be inferred without labeled examples, improving both efficiency and accuracy~\cite{mitchellDetectGPTZeroShotMachineGenerated2023, baoFastDetectGPTEfficientZeroShot2023}. 
Our work is analogous to tasks that employ neural networks for text authorship attribution, but focuses on distinguishing code generated by different LLMs rather than human vs. AI authorship.

\textbf{Characteristics of LLM-Generated Code.} 
Beyond mere detection, understanding intrinsic properties of machine-generated code is crucial. 
LLMs such as ~\cite{openai2023gpt4} and CodeGen~\cite{nijkamp2022codegen} perform autoregressive prediction by learning statistical patterns from large-scale text and code corpora. 
Empirical studies show that LLM-generated code often exhibits repetitive or systematic defect patterns. 
Wang et al.~\cite{wang2025towards} conducted a systematic analysis of generation errors, revealing that LLMs tend to produce complex multi-line mistakes at different locations, originating from various root causes. 
Insights into these characteristics lay the foundation for subsequent code provenance and auditing methods.

\textbf{Code Provenance and Auditing.} 
To ensure system security and traceability, several watermarking- and fingerprint-based approaches have been proposed recently. 
One class of methods introduces biases in a subset of the model vocabulary during generation and later verifies them using statistical tests~\cite{tian2023gptzero, li2023starcoder, wu2023llmdet}. 
In contrast, ~\cite{lee2023wrote} remove low-entropy segments to enhance robustness while reducing code quality degradation. 
These approaches mainly focus on marking or statistical detection at the generation stage, with less attention to analyzing stylistic or semantic properties of machine-generated code itself. 
Our work not only addresses traceability but also systematically summarizes and explains inter-model differences at fine-grained style and semantic levels.

\textbf{Code Similarity Analysis and Representation Learning.}
Code similarity analysis has been a fundamental task in software engineering, with applications ranging from code clone detection to code search. Traditional methods rely on token-based matching, structural analysis, or semantic embeddings. Recent advances leverage contrastive learning for code representation. For instance, CoCoSoDa~\cite{shi2022cocosoda} employs contrastive learning with soft data augmentation to improve code search performance, demonstrating that learning separable code embeddings can effectively capture semantic similarities. CodeMatcher~\cite{liu2020codematcher} combines information retrieval with deep learning to enhance code search by identifying important query terms and utilizing sequence semantics. However, these methods primarily focus on functional similarity rather than stylistic differences. Our approach adapts supervised contrastive learning specifically for capturing stylistic signatures that distinguish different code generation sources, extending beyond functional equivalence to stylistic attribution.

\textbf{Code Style Analysis and Metrics.}
Static code analysis tools such as Checkstyle and Cppcheck examine code style compliance, focusing on naming conventions, formatting, and structural patterns. Research on code style analysis has established metrics for quantifying stylistic properties, including cyclomatic complexity~\cite{mccabe1976complexity}, code smell detection, and maintainability indices. Zakeri-Nasrabadi et al.~\cite{zakeri2023similarity} provide a systematic survey of code similarity metrics and clone detection techniques, highlighting the importance of multi-dimensional style analysis. CodeBLEU~\cite{ren2020codebleu} combines n-gram matching with abstract syntax tree (AST) features for evaluating code synthesis quality. While these tools and metrics are designed for general code quality assessment, they lack specificity for distinguishing LLM-generated code sources. Our CodeStyleAnalyzer extends these concepts by systematically extracting six dimensions of stylistic features tailored for identifying model-specific generation patterns, providing interpretable indicators for code provenance.

\textbf{Contrastive Learning for Code.}
Contrastive learning has shown promise in learning discriminative representations for code-related tasks. Beyond code search, contrastive approaches have been applied to code clone detection, bug detection, and program repair. The core idea is to learn embeddings where similar code samples are close together while dissimilar ones are separated. Supervised contrastive learning~\cite{khosla2020supcon} extends this by leveraging class labels to explicitly pull samples from the same class together. In the context of code, this has been used for learning semantic representations, but not specifically for stylistic attribution across different generation sources. Our work innovatively applies supervised contrastive learning followed by classification fine-tuning to create a separable embedding space that captures model-specific stylistic patterns, enabling accurate multi-class source attribution for LLM-generated code.

\textbf{Understanding Distributional and Model Differences.} 
A series of studies~\cite{dunlap2024describing, zhong2024explaining} used foundational models to describe distributional differences between image datasets. 
~\cite{sunIdiosyncrasiesLargeLanguage2025}  pointed out that the text output of LLMs also exhibits distinguishable patterns.
Our research confirms that these patterns are not only reflected in the style of the text but also in the structure and style of the generated code. The analysis of these differences provides a theoretical basis for code generation attribution and supports empirical studies on subsequent model evaluation, selection, and optimization.


\section{Methodology}
Our approach integrates three complementary components: embedding-based classification, code similarity analysis, and Code Style Analysis. These modules are designed to be synergistic rather than standalone: classification supplies instance-level attribution and a separable representation space; similarity analysis situates these attributions in a global context by quantifying between-model proximity and cluster structures; CodeStyleAnalyzer then explains the drivers of separation with interpretable style and structure indicators. This forms an evidence chain—from signal, to attribution, to interpretation—supporting provenance analysis and comprehensive style characterization.

\subsection{Embedding-based Classification}

Large language models (LLMs) share similar architectures and training approaches—most rely on the Transformer framework \citep{vaswani2017attention}, autoregressive learning objectives \citep{radford2019language}, and overlapping datasets such as GitHub and Stack Overflow. Despite these commonalities, it remains unclear whether different LLMs produce code with consistent stylistic patterns. To explore this, we design a code generation classification task to capture stylistic distinctions among LLM outputs.

\subsubsection{Data Cleaning}

To ensure high-quality training data, we employ a data cleaning strategy involving a script that selects high-quality samples from the collected responses. Specifically, we truncate each code sample to 512 tokens to align with the encoder's input length limit, ensuring stable and efficient training. Additionally, we filter out non-code comments embedded within the code, such as inline explanations or embedded documentation, which are irrelevant to code-style detection and can introduce confounding noise. This selection process filters out low-quality or noisy samples, ensuring that the training set contains representative and diverse code samples that effectively capture stylistic patterns.

\subsubsection{Supervised Contrastive Learning and Classification Training Process}

We employ supervised contrastive learning followed by classification fine-tuning to train our embedding-based classifier, which consists of two complementary training stages. The encoder initially remains frozen, while the projection and classification heads are trainable. The training framework operates as follows:

\textbf{Stage 1: Supervised Contrastive Pre-training:} The projection head and LLM2Vec encoder become trainable during this stage. We employ supervised contrastive loss \citep{khosla2020supcon} to learn representations where samples from the same class cluster together in the embedding space, while samples from different classes are separated. The loss function is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{supcon} = \sum_{i \in I} \frac{-1}{|P(i)|} \sum_{p \in P(i)} \log \frac{\exp(\mathbf{z}_i \cdot \mathbf{z}_p / \tau)}{\sum_{n \in N(i)} \exp(\mathbf{z}_i \cdot \mathbf{z}_n / \tau)},
\end{equation}
where $I$ is the set of all indices in the batch, $P(i)$ is the set of positive indices for anchor $i$ (embeddings $\mathbf{z}_p$), $N(i)$ is the set of negative indices for anchor $i$ (embeddings $\mathbf{z}_n$), $\mathbf{z}_i$ is the normalized embedding of sample $i$, and $\tau$ is a temperature parameter. This contrastive learning approach \citep{chen2020simclr} helps the model capture distinctive stylistic features that distinguish different LLM-generated code samples.

\textbf{Stage 2: Classification Fine-tuning.} Only the classification head remains trainable while the encoder is frozen to preserve the representations learned in Stage 1. We use cross-entropy (CE) loss for fine-tuning on the classification task:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{CE} = -\sum_{i=1}^{N} \sum_{c=1}^{M} y_{i,c} \log(\hat{y}_{i,c}),
\end{equation}
where $N$ is the number of samples, $M$ is the number of classes, $y_{i,c}$ is the true label (1 if sample $i$ belongs to class $c$, 0 otherwise), and $\hat{y}_{i,c}$ is the predicted probability. This stage adapts the classification head to the specific multi-class objective while maintaining the rich stylistic distinctions captured during contrastive pre-training.

\subsubsection{Evaluation and Analysis}

After training, we evaluate the model on a held-out test set, measuring classification accuracy as the primary metric. Additionally, we save the predicted logits and true labels for further analysis, identifying both high-confidence and low-confidence misclassifications to understand the model's behavior and potential failure modes. The projection vectors are exported to support visualization and downstream analysis of the learned representations. 

\subsection{Code Similarity Analysis}
\label{subsec:code_similarity_method}

To compare stylistic similarities of code from different LLMs, we employ TF-IDF vectorization \citep{salton1988term}, which combines local term frequency (TF) with global inverse document frequency (IDF):  

\begin{equation}
    \text{TF-IDF}(t, d) = \text{TF}(t, d) \cdot \text{IDF}(t),
\end{equation}

where
\begin{equation}
    \text{TF}(t, d) = \frac{f_{t,d}}{\sum_{t' \in d} f_{t',d}}, \quad
    \text{IDF}(t) = \log \frac{|D|}{|\{ d \in D : t \in d \}|}.
\end{equation}

Here, $f_{t,d}$ is the frequency of term $t$ in document $d$, $|D|$ is the total number of documents, and $|\{ d \in D : t \in d \}|$ counts documents containing $t$.  

We extract both character-level $n$-grams (3,3), (4,4), (3,5) and word-level $n$-grams (1,1), (1,2). Cosine similarity between vectorized snippets measures stylistic convergence:

\begin{equation}
    \text{cosine}(u, v) = \frac{u \cdot v}{\|u\|\|v\|} \in [-1,1].
\end{equation}

Additionally, we analyze variable naming by lexically extracting identifiers (excluding keywords and built-ins) and computing Top-$k$ frequent distributions to capture model-specific naming tendencies.

\subsection{Code Style Analysis}
\label{subsec:code_style_analysis_method}
To systematically evaluate LLM code style, we develop CodeStyleAnalyzer, an automated tool for static feature extraction and style quantification. It covers six core dimensions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Naming:} identifier naming conventions (camelCase, snake\_case, PascalCase, UPPERCASE, lowercase), average name length, and single-character variable usage.  
    \item \textbf{Comments:} frequency and density of comments (single-line, block, and docstrings).  
    \item \textbf{Syntax:} structural indicators including number of functions/classes, control-flow statements such as \texttt{if}, \texttt{for}, \texttt{while}, and \texttt{try-except}, indentation style, line length, and blank line distribution.  
    \item \textbf{Consistency:} Average spacing in code, including operator spacing and spacing around punctuation, reflecting coding consistency within and across files.  
    \item \textbf{Complexity:} logical complexity via cyclomatic complexity \citep{mccabe1976complexity}, maximum nesting depth, function length, and code size. Cyclomatic complexity is defined as:
    \begin{equation}
        M = E - N + 2P,
    \end{equation}
    where $E$ is the number of edges, $N$ the number of nodes, and $P$ the number of connected components (typically $P=1$). Higher values indicate more complex and less maintainable code.  
    \item \textbf{Patterns:} coding habits including library preferences, exception handling strategies, and function return styles including early return and empty return.  
\end{enumerate}

Finally, each model's analysis results include the \texttt{total\_snippets} statistic, recording the total number of analyzed code snippets to ensure statistical validity in cross-model comparisons.

\section{Experiments and Results}

This work aims to evaluate the robustness of code style detection across multiple large language models (LLMs). The primary task is a multi-class classification problem, where stylistic and structural cues in code are leveraged to distinguish the model of origin.

\subsection{Dataset Construction}

We construct a multi-class dataset tailored for style attribution by aggregating model responses to a shared pool of CodeAlpaca prompts \citep{wangCodeAlpaca2023}. For each prompt, we collect paired completions from multiple mainstream LLMs across several programming languages, ensuring that stylistic differences arise from model behavior rather than task mismatch. Responses are standardized through deterministic formatting, language normalization, and length control to at most 512 tokens to align with encoder limits. We remove boilerplate and non-code explanatory text that is irrelevant to style detection, deduplicate near-identical snippets, and discard malformed or trivially empty outputs. This curation yields a high-quality, label-consistent benchmark that isolates model-specific coding style under matched prompts.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

We use LLM2Vec \citep{behnamghaderLLM2VecLargeLanguage2024} as the base encoder, built on Meta-Llama-3-8B \citep{meta2024llama}. We attach a projection head for supervised contrastive learning to obtain a separable embedding space, and we fine-tune a classification head for $M$-way attribution. Inputs are truncated to 512 tokens for stability and efficiency, and features include textual embeddings and structural patterns (variable naming, function layout, comment usage). Under a unified setup, we report overall accuracy, visualize the learned separable projection, and run a loss ablation comparing supervised contrastive learning plus classification fine-tuning against single-objective baselines. Detailed configurations are in Appendix~\ref{app:implementation_details}.

