\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Effectiveness of Supervised Contrastive Learning}

Our results demonstrate that supervised contrastive learning followed by classification fine-tuning (CGSD) significantly outperforms baseline encoders, achieving 93.25\% attribution accuracy compared to LLM2Vec (60.20\%) and BERT (58.00\%). The ablation study further confirms that both training stages are essential: the full combination achieves 93.25\%, while CE alone yields 60.20\% and SupCon alone reaches 92.25\%. This indicates that contrastive pre-training establishes a separable embedding space (as visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:projection_separation}) where stylistically similar samples cluster together, which classification fine-tuning then leverages for accurate attribution.

\subsection{Stylistic Patterns in LLM-Generated Code}

Character-level n-grams capture the highest similarity across models, indicating shared syntax structures, while word-level features show greater divergence, reflecting distinct lexical choices. Overall similarity remains low (0.01--0.05), confirming that different LLMs produce code with distinct stylistic fingerprints despite architectural similarities. Variable-naming analysis reveals model-specific preferences: Gemini (and often ChatGPT) favors semantic identifiers such as \texttt{number} or \texttt{square}, whereas Claude prefers brevity (e.g., \texttt{num}) and exhibits lower comment density; by contrast, Grok shows higher comment density and more frequent control-flow usage. These consistent patterns suggest intrinsic generative behaviors that are robust to surface-level variations.

\subsection{Practical Implications}

Our findings have practical implications for software development:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Code Provenance:} Stylistic analysis enables reliable identification of code origin.
    \item \textbf{Quality Assessment:} Understanding model-specific patterns helps evaluate code quality and maintainability.
    \item \textbf{Security Auditing:} Stylistic traces can aid in vulnerability detection and code origin verification.
    \item \textbf{Model Selection:} Developers can choose LLMs based on preferred coding style characteristics.
\end{itemize}


\section{Conclusion}

In this paper, we studied stylistic idiosyncrasies in LLM-generated code and proposed the Code Generation Source Detection (CGSD) framework for source attribution and style characterization.

Our experiments demonstrate that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item CGSD achieves 93.25\% attribution accuracy, significantly outperforming baseline encoders (LLM2Vec: 60.20\%, BERT: 58.00\%).
    \item Both supervised contrastive pre-training and classification fine-tuning are essential, with the full combination achieving 93.25\% versus 60.20\% for CE alone.
    \item Supervised contrastive learning effectively learns a separable embedding space where stylistically similar samples cluster together.
    \item Different LLMs exhibit consistent and distinct patterns in naming, commenting, syntax, consistency, and complexity.
\end{itemize}

These results demonstrate that LLM-generated code carries identifiable stylistic fingerprints, enabling accurate source attribution through domain-adapted contrastive learning. Our work provides insights for code provenance, security auditing, and model evaluation, establishing a foundation for accountable AI-driven software development. We will release the curated dataset and reproducible training and analysis scripts upon publication to support deployment and future research.

