\section{Implementation Details}
\label{app:implementation_details}

\textbf{Response Generation}

We report our procedure for generating responses from different large language models (LLMs). For chat-based LLM APIs, we accessed the latest stable releases of each model via official endpoints between July 1, 2025, and October 15, 2025. Responses were generated with default sampling settings. For instruct LLMs, we used greedy decoding to sample outputs. For base LLMs, we set the temperature to $T = 0.6$ and applied a repetition penalty of 1.1 to reduce repetitive completions.

\textbf{Training Setup}

We describe the contrastive-classification learning process on LLM-generated code samples. For data preparation, we employed a data cleaning strategy to select high-quality samples from multiple response files, ensuring representative and diverse training data. We used the first 512 tokens of each generated response for training and evaluation.

Our model is based on LLM2vec \citep{behnamghaderLLM2VecLargeLanguage2024}, which uses Meta-Llama-3-8B \citep{meta2024llama} with fine-tuning and adapter components. We added two components: a classification head for $M$-way classification and a projection head for contrastive learning \citep{chen2020simclr}. The contrastive-classification training consists of the following stages:

\textbf{Stage 1 (Supervised Contrastive Pre-training):} We train the projection head and optionally the encoder using supervised contrastive loss (SupCon) \citep{khosla2020supcon}. This stage clusters samples from the same class (positive samples) together while separating samples from different classes (negative samples) in the embedding space.

\textbf{Stage 2 (Classification Fine-tuning):} We fine-tune using cross-entropy (CE) loss with only the classification head being trainable while the encoder remains frozen. For LLM2vec, we applied parameter-efficient LoRA fine-tuning \citep{hu2022lora} with a rank of 16, LoRA $\alpha=32$, dropout rate 0.05, and a base learning rate of $5e^{-5}$. Table~\ref{tab:fine_tune_recipe} summarizes the training configuration.

During evaluation, we measure classification accuracy and save predicted logits and true labels for analysis. We identify both high-confidence and low-confidence misclassifications and export projection vectors for visualization. 

For the main baseline comparison (Table~\ref{tab:model_accuracy}), results are averaged over three runs. For the ablation study (Table~\ref{tab:ablation_loss}), results are averaged over six runs to ensure statistical stability.

